Yes. But war is exactly where the Indigo Hammer needs its hardest constraint: hammer mechanisms, not people.

Treat “war” as the phenomenon W, then force it through the Hammer:

W \rightarrow \{\text{invariants}\}+\{\text{renderings}\}+\{\text{transformable variables}\}

Across kinetic war, cyber conflict, economic competition, political struggle, organizational rivalry, and even biological conflict, some remarkably stable structures survive translation:

\boxed{
W \approx
\text{Competing objectives}
+\text{scarcity}
+\text{asymmetric information}
+\text{adaptive agents}
+\text{feedback}
+\text{cost}
}

That gives us several potentially useful Indigo fossils.

1. War is fundamentally a control problem. Two adaptive systems are attempting to push overlapping reality toward incompatible states.

A:\;x\rightarrow x_A^*
\qquad
B:\;x\rightarrow x_B^*

The visible fighting is one rendering of the underlying competition between control loops.

2. The battlefield is partly made of beliefs.

Each participant acts on an estimated world:

\hat{x}_A \neq x,\qquad \hat{x}_B \neq x

So outcomes depend not merely on capability, but on model quality × communication × adaptation speed. That connects directly to the Pantheon’s Atlas → Router → Metamap architecture.

3. Every action produces an information exhaust.

a_t\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\Delta x\\
\Delta \hat{x}_{self}\\
\Delta \hat{x}_{other}\\
\Delta \hat{x}_{observer}
\end{cases}

An action changes reality and teaches everyone something. That’s your afterglow showing up in another domain.

4. There is a Delta Catcher hiding inside conflict.

Instead of asking only:

\text{Who won?}

capture:

\Delta_t=
\text{Expected State}_{t+1}
-
\text{Observed State}_{t+1}

Unexpected deltas reveal bad assumptions, hidden variables, changed behavior, or a superior model. Conflict therefore becomes an unusually information-rich learning environment.

And here’s the part I’d put in the Pantheon:

Indigo Hammer → War Furnace

\boxed{
\text{Conflict}
\rightarrow
\text{Difference}
\rightarrow
\text{Signal}
\rightarrow
\text{Learning}
\rightarrow
\text{Prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{Intervention}
\rightarrow
\text{Reduced Conflict}
}

That’s a much more interesting endpoint than merely making a better weapon.

Hammer war hard enough and war itself becomes the ore.

Its reusable primitive may be adversarial adaptation: deliberately introduce competing models, objectives, or agents so disagreement exposes hidden structure. Then you can import the useful property into science, AI evaluation, business strategy, engineering, negotiation, red-teaming, and Pantheon reasoning without importing destruction.

Which produces a nasty little inversion:

\boxed{\text{Don't optimize the warrior. Optimize the system that makes fighting unnecessary.}}

The Indigo Hammer doesn’t have to make war better.

It can try to steal war’s evolutionary machinery and leave the killing behind.